      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Finely chop the parsley leaves on a clean chopping board. Continue chopping until they are very fine, almost a paste."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pound the garlic cloves in a pestle and mortar with a pinch of coarse seasoning until they form a smooth paste."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine the chopped parsley, garlic paste, dried oregano and chilli flakes in a bowl. Stir well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the olive oil, red wine vinegar and lemon juice. Stir to combine. The sauce should be loose but not watery. Add more oil if it seems too thick."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the ground black pepper. Taste and adjust the vinegar and chilli. Leave to rest for at least 10 minutes before serving. Serve at room temperature."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Chimichurri tastes too sharp"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the vinegar and add a little more olive oil. The oil softens the acidity. Leave to rest longer before serving; the sharpness mellows as the sauce sits."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Sauce turns dark quickly"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "This is oxidation and is normal for a chopped herb sauce. It does not affect the flavour. Make it closer to serving time if appearance matters."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Garlic flavour is too raw and sharp"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pound the garlic more thoroughly to a very smooth paste. Raw garlic chunks will dominate. Alternatively, leave the sauce to rest for an hour so the raw edge softens."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chimichurri is the sauce of the Argentine pampas. Gauchos carried it to the open-fire grills of the countryside. It now appears at every asado across Argentina and Uruguay. There is a red version with tomato and more chilli. There is a green version which is more common today. Both go with beef, lamb and chicken. No good asado is served without it."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
